==English==
{{wikipedia|Characterization}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|characterise}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|ML.|characterizare}}, from {{der|en|grc|χαρακτηρίζω||to designate by a characteristic mark}}, from {{m|grc|χαρακτήρ||a mark, character}}. Synchronically analyzable as {{suffix|en|character|ize}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GenAm}} {{IPA|en|/ˈkɛɹəktəɹaɪz/|/ˈkæɹəktəɹaɪz/}}
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈkæɹəktəɹaɪz/}}
* {{hyphenation|en|char|ac|ter|ize}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To depict someone or something a particular way (often negative).
#*{{quote-journal
|en
|date=2019-7-15
|title=The Jewish Case for Open Borders
|author=Greg Afinogenov
|journal=Jewish Currents
|issue=Summer 2019
|url=https://jewishcurrents.org/the-jewish-case-for-open-borders/
|passage=[Stephen] Miller’s uncle, a neuroscientist, has been welcomed onto the public stage for his denunciations of his nephew’s immigration policies, which the elder Miller has '''characterized''' as hypocritical: the Millers’ not-so-distant Jewish ancestors were, of course, immigrants themselves.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To be [[typical]] of.
